# 9. Functional Requirements

### FR-001

Every request body SHALL use a Request Schema.

---

### FR-002

Every response SHALL use a Response Schema.

---

### FR-003

Validation SHALL occur automatically.

---

### FR-004

Every error SHALL follow the standard error schema.

---

### FR-005

Pagination SHALL use a common pagination schema.

---

### FR-006

Schemas SHALL support nested objects.

---

### FR-007

Schemas SHALL support future API versioning.

---

# 10. Standard Response Objects

The following reusable response models SHALL exist:

- Success Response
- Error Response
- Pagination Response
- Empty Response
- List Response
- Metadata Response

---

# 11. Validation Requirements

Validation SHALL include:

- Required fields
- Optional fields
- Type validation
- Enum validation
- Length validation
- Numeric validation
- Custom validators

Future validators SHALL integrate without redesign.

---

# 12. Non Functional Requirements

### NFR-001

Schemas SHALL remain reusable.

---

### NFR-002

Schemas SHALL remain framework-independent except for Pydantic.

---

### NFR-003

Validation SHALL remain deterministic.

---

# 13. Security Requirements

### SEC-001

Schemas SHALL never expose internal models.

---

### SEC-002

Sensitive fields SHALL be excluded.

---

### SEC-003

Internal database identifiers SHALL only be exposed intentionally.

---

### SEC-004

Validation errors SHALL never leak implementation details.

---

# 14. Performance Requirements

### PERF-001

Schema validation SHALL remain lightweight.

---

### PERF-002

Serialization SHALL support future optimization.

---

# 15. Architecture Constraints

Schemas SHALL NOT:

- Access databases.
- Call services.
- Contain business logic.
- Perform authorization.
- Modify application state.

Schemas exist solely for validation and serialization.

